# Descending a floor mid-run, and what does and does not cross the boundary.
#
# The swap happens between ticks, so the tick that asked still finished over the
# tree it started with (I8). What a replay has to reproduce is not just "a load
# happened" but everything below.

# Before: floor one, full health, and a variable the script has been counting up.
tick 4 /Floor1/Hero var:floor_name == Floor1
tick 4 /Floor1/Hero var:hp == 20
tick 4 /Floor1/Hero var:depth == 1
tick 4 /Floor1/Hero var:ticks_here == 5

# After: a different tree, reached through `scene.request_load` at tick 5.
tick 8 /Floor2/Hero var:floor_name == Floor2

# The carry crossed. 20 - 3 is the whole point of handing a payload through the
# load rather than stashing it in a global the hash cannot see.
tick 8 /Floor2/Hero var:hp == 17
tick 8 /Floor2/Hero var:depth == 2

# And what did *not* cross. `ticks_here` was 5 when the floor changed and is
# counting again from the new tree's first tick, because script variables name
# nodes that no longer exist. A swap that kept them would read 8 here.
tick 8 /Floor2/Hero var:ticks_here == 3

# The run continued rather than restarting: the loot stream is further along, so
# the second floor's roll differs from the first's. A swap that reset the RNG
# would produce 427 twice and every floor would be generated from the same
# numbers.
tick 4 /Floor1/Hero var:roll == 427
tick 8 /Floor2/Hero var:roll == 353
